+# QA report: "You're subscribed" shown but no email arrives
+
+**Date:** 2026-11-11 · **Build:** `newsletter@1.2.0` (commit `3216ca3`) · **Browser:** Chromium via Playwright
+
+## Summary
+
+The signup page says **"You're subscribed. Check your inbox for the first issue."** even when the server **rejected** the address with `422`. Those people were never added to the subscriber list, so they never get an email. The page doesn't tell them anything went wrong.
+
+## Root cause
+
+`public/app.js` sends the `fetch` request but never looks at the response:
+
+```js
+await fetch('/api/subscribe', { ... });          // response discarded
+status.textContent = "You're subscribed. ...";   // runs for 201 AND 422
+form.reset();                                     // also clears what they typed
+```
+
+`fetch` only rejects on a network failure. An HTTP `422` resolves normally, so the success message always shows. The `catch` branch only covers the case where the server can't be reached at all.
+
+## Who is affected
+
+These addresses pass the browser's own `type="email"` check, so the request is sent, but `validate()` in `server.js` rejects them:
+
+| Address entered            | API response                                       | Page shows       |
+|----------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|------------------|
+| `reader@example.com`       | `201 {"ok":true}`                                  | You're subscribed ✅ |
+| `Jane+news@example.com`    | `422 {"error":"Addresses with + are not supported."}` | You're subscribed ❌ |
+| `a@localhost`              | `422 {"error":"Enter a valid email address."}`     | You're subscribed ❌ |
+| `a@example.xn--p1ai` (IDN TLD in punycode) | `422 {"error":"Enter a valid email address."}` | You're subscribed ❌ |
+| `x@example.c`              | `422 {"error":"Enter a valid email address."}`     | You're subscribed ❌ |
+
+The largest real-world group is probably **plus-addressing** (`name+tag@gmail.com` and similar), which many people use for newsletters. Internationalized TLDs are also rejected because the server's TLD pattern is `[a-z]{2,}`.
+
+## Steps to reproduce
+
+1. `node server.js`, then open `http://localhost:3000/`.
+2. Enter `jane+news@example.com` and click **Subscribe**.
+3. In DevTools → Network, `POST /api/subscribe` returns `422` with `{"error":"Addresses with + are not supported."}`.
+4. The page still says "You're subscribed. Check your inbox…" and clears the field.
+
+**Expected:** The page shows the server's error, does not claim success, and keeps the typed address so the person can correct it.
+
+## Regression test
+
+`e2e/subscribe.test.js` uses `node:test` and Playwright. It starts `server.js` on an ephemeral port, submits addresses through the real form, and checks the API status and the on-page message.
+
+```
+node --test e2e/subscribe.test.js
+```
+
+Current result: **1 pass, 2 fail.**
+- ✅ control: a plain address gets a `201` and a success message
+- ❌ `jane+news@example.com`: server returns `422`, but the page says "You're subscribed…"
+- ❌ `ops@localhost`: server returns `422`, but the page says "You're subscribed…"
+
+The failing tests also require the page to show the server's `error` text.
+
+As a check, I ran the same test file against a scratch copy where `app.js` checks `res.ok` and shows `error`. All 3 tests passed, so the test fails because of this bug and not because of a setup problem. The app in this repo was **not** changed.
+
+## Suggested fix (not applied)
+
+- **Client (the bug):** In `app.js`, read the response. If `!res.ok`, show `(await res.json()).error` and don't reset the form. Show the success message only on a 2xx response.
+- **Product decision:** Decide whether `+` addresses and non-ASCII/punycode TLDs should be rejected at all. Rejecting plus-addressing turns away real, deliverable addresses.
+
+## Other observations (outside this bug)
+
+- Subscribers are kept only in an in-memory `Set` in `server.js`. Every restart loses them, and the server has no code that sends email. If anyone is supposed to receive a "first issue" or confirmation email, that happens outside this repo or not at all. Worth checking separately.
+- The test runs 2 of the rejected cases, plus-addressing and a domain with no TLD. The other rejected inputs in the table go through the same client code path.
